comments[7]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-15T08:35:00.796Z","harness:claude-code","Verification evidence (2026-11-15, branch feat/stale-work-visibility-and-takeover). The isolated scenarios in `tests/unit/commands/lifecycle/claim-stale-takeover.spec.ts` pin only the wall clock, so the real lock, history and CLI paths run:\n\n- An active claim (5h idle) refuses with `stale_claim_takeover_refused`. The refusal carries `claim_activity` and a specific `required` text, names the claim's `eligible_at`, and leaves the history stream byte-identical.\n- An idle claim (96h) is taken over. `takeover` and `context.stale_claim_takeover` match field by field, and the warning reads `claim_stale_takeover:agent-a->agent-b:idle_hours=96`.\n- A holder that renews at T+100h makes a takeover at T+101h refuse (`idle_hours` 1, `claim_age_hours` 101).\n- With `--if-available`, an active claim is skipped and a stale one is taken over.\n- Unclaimed and self-held items behave as plain claims (no `takeover` key).\n- A plain `already_claimed_by` names `--takeover-stale` in its next steps only once the claim is stale.\n- `--force` and `--next` combinations refuse before any write.\n- `pm claim --start` and `start-task` take over and start. `takeoverStale` does not leak into the status update (register-commands spec).\n- `pm next` `held_by_others` rows carry `claimed_at`, `idle_hours` and `stale`.\n- No valid activity time means fail-closed: never stale, and the next steps point at history.\n\nParity: CLI, MCP (strict schema 4.28.0, with `takeoverStale` on claim and start-task plus mutual-exclusion groups) and the SDK (`runClaim` and `runStartTask`). Completions cover bash, zsh and fish. The flag lexicon budget is claim 40 and start-task 24, and the help baselines were raised by hand without an inventory rewrite.\n\nNarrowed row: `last_activity_at` was removed from `NextHeldRow` because the execute intent's bounded read lost two `decision_needed` rows to the wider held rows. The exact timestamp stays in the refusal. The calibration re-approval moves next from 9 to 8 rows, keeps all 5 ready rows, and makes the session spend fewer tokens.\n\n`pm test pm-gh1336 --run` passed (run test-local-mwhkv9vw-6tp7ji).\n"
  "2026-11-15T09:03:19.063Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 (gpt-6.1-sol xhigh, PR #1734 head 229317c4a) returned 5 findings. I verified each and fixed all 5 in the follow-up commit:\n\n1. **[P1] Corrupt history made the takeover fail open.** Old metadata plus a recent history entry followed by a malformed line made the history read return empty, so the takeover was judged from `updated_at` alone.\n   - Root cause: the clock took the newest of `updated_at` and history.\n   - Fix: the clock is now `updated_at` itself. Every recorded mutation advances it, and the merge driver resolves it to the later side. History stands in only when `updated_at` does not parse, and unreadable history in that fallback yields no activity, so the claim is never judged stale.\n2. **[P2] Flat MCP `start-task` dropped `takeoverStale`.** Fix: `HOISTED_ACTION_OPTION_KEYS` gains a `start-task` entry. A transport test now drives `runAction` for both claim and start-task.\n3. **[P2] Sweeps and health disagreed** when history was newer than metadata. Fixed by the same clock change: `--updated-before` now filters on exactly the value the advisories judge.\n4. **[P2] Status ids were unquoted in remedies**, so `doing(done)` broke the shell. Fix: sweeps use `renderPmCommand`, and `return_to_open` uses `quoteCommandArg`.\n5. **[P3] A threshold accepted by settings (3e9 hours) threw a RangeError** in `eligible_at`. Fix: `staleEligibleAt` returns null past the Date range.\n\nThe clock change also makes health and context read history only for items whose `updated_at` does not parse.\n"
  "2026-11-15T09:19:49.759Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 2 on PR #1734 (Greptile, on heads 79de4c330 and a8dc9c7e4) returned two P1 findings. Both are verified and fixed:\n\n1. **Idle health rows copied the stored assignee verbatim**, so a holder identity that an older version stored with URL userinfo leaked into `details.in_progress_work`. Fix: the classifier redacts it with `redactIdentityCredentials`, matching the GH-1707 rule for claim refusals. `pm next` `held_by_others` rows redact the same way. Specs plant a legacy credentialed holder directly in the item file.\n2. **Restored claims looked abandoned** once round 1 reduced the clock to `updated_at`. `pm restore` writes the historical document, keeping its old `updated_at`, and appends a new `restore` entry. Fix: the clock is again the newer of `updated_at` and the latest history entry, so a restore counts as activity. Codex round 1 finding #1 (corrupt history failing open) is now fixed directly instead of through the clock:\n   - A history stream that exists but cannot be parsed marks `claim_activity.history_unreadable` and is never judged stale.\n   - The refusal says the stream could not be read and names `pm history <id> --verify`.\n\nRound 1 finding #3 (sweep vs health mismatch) stands as documented behaviour: the `--updated-before` sweeps are candidate lists by metadata time, a superset when history runs ahead, and the takeover re-judges with the full clock. New scenarios cover a holder's restore and a corrupt stream refusing the takeover.\n"
  "2026-11-15T09:46:23.099Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 3 on PR #1734 (Greptile on c800079e8, CodeRabbit full review). All findings were verified, and each is fixed:\n\n- **Greptile P1: compaction made abandoned claims look active.** The fresh `history_compact` entry reset the idle time.\n  - Fix: the clock counts only substantive history entries, using the existing `classifyHistoryEvent` policy. `history_compact`, `history_repair`, `history_redact` and the other maintenance ops are excluded, and `restore` still counts.\n- **CodeRabbit: a `null` history line escaped the advisory fallback.**\n  - Fix: `latestHistoryTimestamp` skips decoded values that are not records.\n  - Ownership evidence treats a stream with any non-record line (`null`, a string, a record without `ts` or `patch`) as unreadable, so the takeover fails closed.\n- **CodeRabbit: idle rows held by another agent had no working remedy.**\n  - Fix: `item_remedies` gains `take_over_idle: pm claim <id> --takeover-stale`, while `pm claim <id>` stays for unowned work.\n- **CodeRabbit, docs:**\n  - The unreadable-history case is the refusal code `stale_claim_takeover_refused`, with `claim_activity.history_unreadable` as evidence.\n  - The lock serializes the takeover with renewals. A renewal recorded before the takeover takes the lock refuses it; that is not \"renewal always wins\".\n- **CodeRabbit, tracker:**\n  - pm-7zs0's elapsed reminder is re-armed for 2026-11-16T07:00Z with its original text.\n  - pm-td81nb's acceptance criteria now cover main advancing before sealing.\n"
  "2026-11-15T10:24:55.446Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 3 (gpt-6.1-sol xhigh, head d99a53a6d) returned five verified findings. All are fixed:\n\n1. **[P1] Compaction erased substantive activity.** A restore folded into a `history_compact_baseline` was discarded as maintenance, so the old `updated_at` made the claim look idle (1h became 97h).\n   - Fix: the baseline counts as activity. It carries the time of the last pruned entry, which bounds the work it replaced. The `history_compact` audit entry (compaction time) still does not count.\n2. **[P2] Record validation was too loose.** A record without `op`, or with patch entries that were null or lacked a string `path`, slipped through and was skipped (failing open) or crashed the classifier.\n   - Fix: one shared `isWellFormedHistoryRecord` validator (string `ts` and `op`, every patch operation a record with a string `path`). Advisory reads skip anything else, and the takeover treats the stream as unreadable.\n3. **[P2] Remedies ignored the selected tracker.** Fix: they now carry `--pm-path` through `resolvePmCommandScopeArgs` when the diagnosed tracker was selected explicitly or via `PM_PATH`.\n4. **[P2] Unowned detail rows published metadata-only activity.** Fix: the scan reads history for every unowned in-progress item, since its row is published, so `age_hours` and `last_activity_at` are exact. Owned items still skip history while `updated_at` is fresh.\n5. **[P2] Markdown context dropped the new counts.** Fix: `renderContextMarkdown` prints `in_progress_unowned` and `in_progress_idle` beside `in_progress` when they are non-zero.\n\nExact 100% coverage passed locally on d99a53a6d (12,816 tests), and focused coverage of the touched modules is 100/100/100/100.\n"
  "2026-11-15T10:54:24.663Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 4 (head 25f2fef3a) found one issue: the stale judgement read history under the embedded id, while the store locks and appends under the storage id taken from the file name. On a case-sensitive filesystem, an item stored as PM-A with an embedded id pm-a would have been judged from the missing pm-a stream, so it would read as idle. Fix: mutateItem's callback receives an ItemMutationLocation {storageId} as a new optional second argument; existing one-argument callbacks are unaffected. The claim reads the stream the lock guards. A spec drives the decision with a storage id whose stream holds recent work and a differently-cased embedded id. Exact coverage passed on 25f2fef3a (12,817 tests)."
  "2026-11-15T11:01:25.303Z","harness:claude-code","Bot review on head f09768f34 (Greptile and a CodeRabbit full review) found three valid issues. All are fixed:\n\n1. **Greptile: repeated compaction renewed abandoned claims.** A baseline's timestamp is the time of its last pruned entry, and a second compaction folds the first compaction's audit entry into its prefix, so maintenance time came back.\n   - Fix: compaction now records the prefix's newest substantive time as `context.history_compaction.last_activity_at` (null when the prefix held no work), carried through nested baselines. The clock reads that value.\n   - A legacy baseline without the field still counts with its own timestamp, a conservative upper bound.\n   - An integration test compacts twice and keeps the original work time.\n2. **CodeRabbit: a well-formed record with an unparseable timestamp kept the stream readable.** The record was skipped, so the takeover could fail open. Fix: the takeover requires every record to carry a parseable timestamp, otherwise the stream is unreadable.\n3. **CodeRabbit: a nested snake_case `takeover_stale` did not override the flat `takeoverStale` on MCP claim.** Fix: claim and start-task join the actions whose declared nested snake_case options are renamed to camelCase, which also covers nested `if_available` and the other claim parameters.\n"
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-15T07:37:48.377Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation decisions (2026-11-15):\n\n- **Clock.** One activity clock: the newest of `updated_at` and the newest history entry. Every recorded mutation advances `updated_at`; I verified create, comment_add, note_add, files_add and claim on a sandbox. History covers trackers whose metadata and stream diverged. A claim with no valid activity time is never stale (fail-closed).\n- **Threshold.** The existing `mutation_guard.stale_in_progress_hours` (default 72), reused rather than adding a second knob. The item health calls stale is the item `--takeover-stale` may take.\n- **Race safety.** The decision runs inside the claim's `mutate` callback under the item lock. A holder's renewal is a mutation that has to wait for that lock, so it either lands before the decision (and is seen) or after the takeover.\n- **Evidence.** `mutateItemWithHistoryContextResolver` seals `context.stale_claim_takeover` into the same claim entry. The result carries the same receipt as `takeover`.\n- **Combinations.** `--takeover-stale` with `--force` and with `--next` are both refused (`ambiguous_option_combination`) before any read. `--if-available` turns an active-claim refusal into a skip.\n- **Ownership-source split.** `resolveItemOwnershipActivity` always returns activity, so the claim path carries no unreachable optional branches. `resolveItemOwnershipConflict` keeps its signature.\n- **pm next.** It reads one stream per returned `held_by_others` row (bounded by `--limit`), never per held item in the corpus. Bulk occupancy stays with pm-1wujzi."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-15T07:37:51.949Z","harness:claude-code","A new field on a mutation result is invisible on `pm <cmd> --json` until `src/core/output/mutation-projection.ts` lets it through. The lean mutation envelope copies only allowlisted plain-object keys (`recovery`, `follow_up`, `handoff`, `completion`, `start`, and now `takeover`), so the SDK result had the receipt while the CLI JSON silently dropped it. Check every new result key against the CLI JSON, not just the SDK return value."
